# Gray-flow User-Agent contract (iOS)

## 1. Baseline rules (apply to every gray-flow build)

The forged User-Agent is used by BOTH the HTTP client that hits the config
endpoint (`secure_agent.dart`) AND the WebView (`setUserAgent` in
`content_browser.dart`). It must:

- **Look like a real Mobile Safari on a real iPhone.** Real iOS version,
  a current Safari `Version/…`, `AppleWebKit/605.1.15`, `Mobile/15E148`,
  `Safari/604.1`.
- **NEVER contain** any of these substrings (case-insensitive):
  - `Dart` / `Flutter` (Flutter's default UA leaks this)
  - `CFNetwork` / `Darwin` (iOS `URLSession` default UA leaks these — you
    MUST override it on the HTTP client)
  - `WebView` / `wv`
  - the app's bundle id, class names, or SDK identifiers
- **Reflect the actual device.** Query `device_info_plus` for the iOS
  release / model; do not hardcode a single device string. Ten identical
  UAs across ten installs are a cluster signal for scanners.
- **Be identical between HTTP + WebView.** The partner backend cross-checks
  the two; a mismatch breaks session continuity.
- **Bump the iOS + Safari version per project.** A stale version (iOS 15
  on an app shipping in 2026) is trivially detectable, and an identical
  build string across sibling apps is a cross-app fingerprint.

### Version rule for AI agents
- Pin a **current major iOS** (e.g. `18` or the latest shipping major) and
  a matching Safari `Version/<major>.<minor>`.
- Vary the minor / patch per project — never copy the exact triple from a
  sibling app or the template default.
- `AppleWebKit/605.1.15`, `Mobile/15E148`, `Safari/604.1` are stable
  Safari tokens and can stay constant.

Canonical shape (no identity suffix):

```
Mozilla/5.0 (iPhone; CPU iPhone OS 18_5 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/605.1.15 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/18.5 Mobile/15E148 Safari/604.1
```

Source any version fragments through `feather_codec.dart` (`unfoldFeathers(...)`),
never as plaintext literals. Keep a hardcoded fallback UA used ONLY if
`device_info_plus` throws — and make sure the fallback also obeys every
rule above.

---

## 2. Identity suffix — slot vs crash games

Some partner backends require the app identity appended to the User-Agent
so their attribution / cashier routing works. **Whether the suffix is
required depends on the game theme, not on the partner:**

| Game theme | Examples | Append `appid/… appname/…`? |
|---|---|---|
| **Slot games** | Zeus, Magma, fruit-machine, roulette, poker, book-of-*, wheel-of-*, mines with a slot skin, lucky-spinner | **YES — required** |
| **Crash games** | Aviator / rocket, plane, jetX, spaceman, balloon, coin-flip crash, multiplier-tap crash | **NO — do not append** |

If the brief does not make the category unambiguous, **STOP and ask the
user**. Guessing is wrong — a suffix on a crash build corrupts the
partner's tracking and gets the account flagged.

### Slot-game suffix — exact format
Append after the standard browser UA, space-separated, last:
```
<browser UA> appid/<bundleId> appname/<AppNameToken>
```
- `appid/` — the bundle id, exactly `EraHatchConfig.bundleId`.
- `appname/` — the app name **without spaces** (PascalCase, e.g.
  `EggRunnerAdventure`). Do NOT URL-encode; do NOT lowercase.

Full example (slot build):
```
Mozilla/5.0 (iPhone; CPU iPhone OS 18_5 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/605.1.15 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/18.5 Mobile/15E148 Safari/604.1 appid/com.super.game appname/GameNaming
```

Rules:
- Must be the **very last segment** — after `Safari/604.1`.
- Source `bundleId` and app name from `EraHatchConfig` — never hardcode at the
  call site.
- Same augmented UA on HTTP + WebView (§1).
- If the theme later flips slot ↔ crash (rebrand), the suffix must be
  added/removed and a fresh build shipped — the value is cached
  server-side per install.

---

## 3. Decision procedure for AI agents

1. Locate the game brief / theme in the customer request.
2. Match it against §2:
   - Clearly slot → include the suffix.
   - Clearly crash → omit the suffix.
   - Ambiguous / missing → do not scaffold; ask one batched question:
     > Is this a **slot** game (spinning reels / roulette / cards) or a
     > **crash** game (rising multiplier / aviator / rocket)? It changes
     > the User-Agent we must ship — I need to know before continuing.
3. Write the decision as a code comment above the UA builder in
   `secure_agent.dart`:
   ```dart
   // GAME THEME CATEGORY: slot  (appid/appname suffix REQUIRED)
   // — or —
   // GAME THEME CATEGORY: crash (no appid/appname suffix)
   ```

---

## 4. Verification (before shipping)

- [ ] Print the built UA in a debug run — matches the canonical shape (§1).
- [ ] Suffix presence matches the game theme (§2).
- [ ] Same UA on HTTP client and WebView.
- [ ] Grep the release build's strings for `Dart`, `Flutter`, `CFNetwork`,
      `Darwin`, `WebView`, and the version from the previous project — all
      must be absent.
- [ ] Load `https://www.whatismybrowser.com/detect/what-is-my-user-agent`
      in the WebView — it must identify as Safari on iPhone with the
      current major.

If any check fails, do not ship — fix the UA builder first.
